{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000ca51","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a071e0-fd9d-7f63-93c7-0936cc7c93be"},"sequence":899,"recorded_at":1780531400001793,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"96f19a54-66b9-47e5-b9f8-d199deceb070","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"96f19a54-66b9-47e5-b9f8-d199deceb070","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"96f19a54-66b9-47e5-b9f8-d199deceb070"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"25b17f74-f024-4a4f-8288-3d80e9bcd378"},"task_id":"25b17f74-f024-4a4f-8288-3d80e9bcd378","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"25b17f74-f024-4a4f-8288-3d80e9bcd378","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-1snnef/TASK-260830-1snnef_review6-verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-1snnef — review round 6 — ACCEPTED\n2|\n3|CR `CR-TASK-260830-1snnef-7` revision 7. Base `57afcc6d`, candidate tree\n4|`771f9c53`. Working tree re-derived through an isolated index at the start and\n5|after every mutant battery: `771f9c53fb708e5b401df371899c0d316bfd8e61`, equal\n6|to the declared candidate. `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`\n7|md5 `a0855df114dab02dcdbd55f8e48a0145` before and after the hand-applied T01.\n8|\n9|Verdict: **accepted**. Nothing blocking. One stated bound recorded below; it is\n10|a corpus bound in a production-dead branch, not a bypass, and I measured the\n11|production gate over its whole domain rather than inferring it.\n12|\n13|## Gates\n14|\n15|| Gate | Result |\n16|| --- | --- |\n17|| `go build ./...` | exit 0 |\n18|| `go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n19|| `gofmt -l internal/` | empty |\n20|| `go test ./... -count=1` | 14/14 packages ok |\n21|| `go test ./internal/terminalbackend/ -cover` | 90.3% |\n22|\n23|## G-A — the replacement corpus, judged as the corpus\n24|\n25|**Derived, not a longer hand list.** `TestSurrogateGateAgreesWithCanonicalJSON`\n26|(`internal_pin_test.go:297`) enumerates every code point in U+D800..U+DFFF as a\n27|lone escape (2048 vectors) and every code point in that range as the *second*\n28|unit after a fixed high surrogate (2048 vectors), asserting admit-iff-low. The\n29|old 10-vector table is gone. Every dropped vector is behaviourally subsumed:\n30|`\"\\ud800abc\"` and `\"\\ud800\"` traverse the same `!ok` path in\n31|`readUTF16EscapeUnit`, and `\"\\udc00\\ud800\"` returns at the same lone-low arm as\n32|`\"\\udc00\"`.\n33|\n34|**Both bounds, both directions.** I mutated each of the four numeric bounds in\n35|the escape arm and each in both directions where meaningful. Every one is RED,\n36|and the failure counts are the arithmetic you would predict from the width of\n37|the hole, which is the check that the sweep is actually enumerating rather than\n38|tripping on one lucky vector:\n39|\n40|| Mutant | Site | Result |\n41|| --- | --- | ---: |\n42|| low upper `0xdfff` → `0xdc00` | `manifest.go:307` | RED, 1023 failures |\n43|| high upper `0xdbff` → `0xd900` | `manifest.go:302` | RED, 768 |\n44|| high lower `0xd800` → `0xd900` | `manifest.go:302` | RED, 1543 |\n45|| pair-continuation upper `>0xdfff` → `>0xdc00` | `manifest.go:304` | RED, 1024 |\n46|| pair-continuation lower `<0xdc00` → `<0xdd00` | `manifest.go:304` | RED, 257 |\n47|\n48|The high-surrogate bound you had not measured is killed from both sides.\n49|\n50|**Valid pairs still admit — the gate is not one-sided.** Driven through the\n51|production decoder `decodeStrictObject`, not the helper: escaped `😀`,\n52|raw UTF-8 U+1F600, both corner pairs `𐀀` and `􏿿`, CJK, and\n53|two adjacent pairs are all admitted. The complementary direction is already\n54|pinned in the battery: r5bat `B4` (gate widened so a valid pair is rejected too)\n55|is RED.\n56|\n57|**The raw WTF-8 arm is the intended one and is witnessed.** `decodeStrictObject`\n58|(`manifest.go:374`) runs `utf8.Valid(raw)` → `document encoding` *before*\n59|`hasLoneSurrogateEscape` → `document surrogate escape`. Raw WTF-8 is not valid\n60|UTF-8, so `document encoding` is correct and inevitable, not a slide.\n61|The inventory carries that row at `manifest.go / decodeStrictObject /\n62|CodeMismatch / \"document encoding\" #1`, witnessed by\n63|`TestManifestEncodingRefusals`; `TestDocumentWTF8SurrogateRefused` asserts that\n64|exact detail string at all three production entries (`ParseProbe`,\n65|`ParseManifest`, `ParseEvidence`), so a slide to another arm reddens it. The\n66|surrogate arm keeps its own separate witness on the escape road\n67|(`TestDocumentSurrogateEscapeRefused`), and r5bat `B1` (surrogate call deleted\n68|from `decodeStrictObject`) is RED through it. The two roads are not proving each\n69|other.\n70|\n71|**The agreement test now catches the disagreement it missed.** Disabling\n72|`isWTF8SurrogateAt` entirely reddens `TestSurrogateGateAgreesWithCanonicalJSON`\n73|with 6 agreement failures — the local gate admitting what `canonicaljson`\n74|refuses. That is the exact defect you reported, now caught.\n75|\n76|### Stated bound (not blocking)\n77|\n78|Asked directly: the WTF-8 vector set inside that test is **hand-written**, not\n79|derived — 4 positive encodings (ED A0 80, ED B0 80, ED BF BF, ED AF 93), one\n80|negative neighbour (ED 9F BF), two backslash-adjacency cases. That is 4 of the\n81|2048 encodings in the branch's domain. All four *bound* narrowings I threw at\n82|`isWTF8SurrogateAt` die on those corners, but a **punch-out** mutant\n83|(`raw[index+1] != 0xa1`, admitting the 64 encodings of U+D840..U+D87F) survives\n84|the entire package. Same shape as the original finding, one branch over.\n85|\n86|I did not route it as a finding, for two measured reasons rather than an\n87|argument:\n88|\n89|1. **The gate is right over its whole domain.** A derived reviewer sweep of all\n90|   2048 encodings ED A0..BF 80..BF against the production predicate:\n91|   `missed = 0 of 2048`. Against the neighbouring valid band ED 80..9F\n92|   (U+D000..U+D7FF): `false positives = 0 of 2048`.\n93|2. **The branch is production-dead, and I proved it rather than reading it.**\n94|   Disabling `isWTF8SurrogateAt` fails *only* the white-box pin — no production\n95|   test moves — because `utf8.Valid` has already refused every raw WTF-8 document\n96|   at `document encoding`. leaf2 `M81` (delete that `utf8.Valid` arm) is RED, so\n97|   the arm that actually closes the class is itself measured.\n98|\n99|So the residual is: a defence-in-depth branch no production entry reaches,\n100|whose corpus measures 0.2% of its domain while the branch itself is correct at\n101|100%. Worth deriving the sweep the next time this file is opened; not worth a\n102|seventh round. Logged, and flagged to the sibling Story since it carries the\n103|same surrogate-gate copy.\n104|\n105|## G-B — round 5's three findings\n106|\n107|**F1 — confirmed.** `TestCheckProviderDescriptorGenerationBounds` gained\n108|`{\"invalid utf-8\", \"gen\\xff\"}`. Dropping the `!utf8.ValidString(generation)`\n109|conjunct at `terminalbackend.go:606` now reddens\n110|`TestCheckProviderDescriptorGenerationBounds/invalid_utf-8` and nothing else.\n111|r5bat2 `S11` RED (was the finding), `S12` RED.\n112|\n113|**F2 — confirmed, and the kill is behavioural, not inventory inflation.** The\n114|new `malformed digest on both sides` subtest puts `\"not-a-digest\"` on the\n115|descriptor **and** the binding, so the equality arm (#2) cannot fire on equal\n116|values and only the `scalar.ParseDigest` shape arm (#1, `terminalbackend.go:587`)\n117|can refuse. To keep the AST inventory out of the measurement I neutralised the\n118|arm *without deleting it* (`err != nil && false`), leaving the arm count and\n119|every literal intact: exactly one test fails,\n120|`TestCheckProviderDescriptorBindingDigestMismatch/malformed_digest_on_both_sides`,\n121|and both inventory directions stay green. Arm #1 now dies to behaviour.\n122|\n123|**F3 — confirmed.** The README now says the inventory \"requires every production\n124|refusal arm to be **declared** with a resolving named asserting test in both\n125|directions; behavioural proof stays with each row's named test, which the\n126|inventory resolves textually\". That is what it guarantees. The file's own header\n127|states the same bound plus the kill-inflation warning.\n128|\n129|## G-C — resurrections\n130|\n131|Production moved in exactly one file since round 5 (`manifest.go`, +32 lines:\n132|the WTF-8 branch); `terminalbackend.go` and `conformance.go` are byte-identical,\n133|and the test deltas are additive except the pin corpus replacement analysed\n134|above. I re-ran every inherited battery anyway.\n135|\n136|| Battery | Round 5 | Round 6 |\n137|| --- | --- | --- |\n138|| leaf 2 bat1 (60, `manifest.go`) | 44 K / 0 S / 12 CF / 4 AB | **identical** |\n139|| leaf 2 bat2 (47, `manifest.go`) | 47 K / 0 S | **47 K / 0 S** |\n140|| leaf 1 (71, `terminalbackend.go`) | 60 K / 8 S / 3 n-a | **identical**, survivors id-for-id |\n141|| leaf 1 re-anchor (N03r–R07r) | 5 RED | **5 RED** |\n142|| conformance bat1+2+3 (48) | 46 K / 1 S / 1 unmeasurable | **identical** |\n143|| round-5 new (r5bat/2/3, 29) | 26 RED, S10+B5 GREEN, S11 finding | **28 RED, S10+B5 GREEN** |\n144|| T01 (hand, ownership registry) | KILLED | **KILLED** |\n145|\n146|**Zero resurrections. Zero regressions.** Survivor sets reproduce id-for-id:\n147|leaf 1 `N09, N13, N14, N15, X01, X02, X04, X10`; conformance `C45` (the\n148|disclosed unbounded-domain bound); leaf 2 none; r5bat `S10` (equivalent mutant)\n149|and `B5` (intended-GREEN control). Unmeasurable reproduce too: leaf 1 `M32`\n150|(build error), `N12` and `N03/N04/N05` (anchor drift, all five re-anchored and\n151|RED), `T01` (harness anchor; applied by hand and RED), conformance `C08`\n152|(non-compiling; `C08b` RED).\n153|\n154|`T01` is the one I re-ran by hand rather than trusting: it is the self-minted\n155|acceptance-case declaration gate, and `ownership.v0.5.0.json` grew by 141